opedia and perhaps also makes more sense in terms of gameplay if it doesn't create issues hopefully, trying it to see anyways etc, see sevopedia entries or/and project/mod docs for details[NEWLINE]- Shrines now also require a tech to be built, which is their religion's tech requirement. This allows to make shrines to also appear in tech tree, plus now shrines cannot be built without also having the religion's tech matching (for example during conquest or such, anyways etc), since shrines are not special buildings unlike temple, monastery, cathedral, maybe this is a fine even desirable way to handle it (in tech tree display, also about the conquest thing or similar or other issues or examples of what may happen but anyways etc as these are world wonders unlike the temple, the monastery, and the cathedral), tech prerequirement for shrines was NONE. See also docs for details or related issues/fixes/reworks ('s) info anyways etc.[NEWLINE]- Removed religion "confucianism", added [LINK=RELIGION_PAGANISM]religion "paganism"[\LINK], more popular and relevant for civ4 maybe as many leaders were religious if i am not mistaken but with no religion, see notes_about_religious_design.txt for details in the docs online on github or(/and(anyways etc)) offline in this project files too for details[NEWLINE]- Fixed and ajusted many religious inaccuracies of favourite religions, at least those i spotted with (being) certainty (certain enough) maybe anwyas etc (or not or/and other or/and not anways etc), for example frederick the great was agnostic not christian, brennus more leaning non religous than pagan (not inaccurate since paganism didn't exist but may fix other xml related fields or maybe not such as religion preference or not anwyays etc), and genghis khan, while tengrist something and not strictly daoist, expressed or/and showed(?) a strong respect(/liking?) for (a(/the?)) daoist leader, so his favourite religion can be assessed to be daoism, especially if he had to choose between civ4 only religions, here being none or daoism, he leans more strongly -much mroe if i am not mistake but anyways etc) towrds daoism), so a leader can have a favourite religion without it being their religion of themselves, see notes mentionned above for details, and this is all if i am not mistaken, but (i thnk)(and hope but anyways etc) if this is (and i was) accurate enouh (but anyways etc) hopefuly accurate enough and (much maybe(?) maybe or not or/and other or not anyways etc) accurate now hopefully maybe or not or/and other or/and not (but) anwyays etc[NEWLINE]- Handicap (i.e. difficulty) settings are now gradually harder, lower/lowest handicap being harder, and highest difficulties being less of a grind and hard in their own ways at least in theory, see docs for details and status update on these or/and xml for details.[NEWLINE]- Other handicap settings changed in other ways, for example unit hammer costs and tech (todo) beaker/gold cost the same at all difficulties for the human player for easier calculations and strategy, please view docs or/and xml or/and handicap data tables for updated status and info if any more than this line, thanks[NEWLINE]- Barbarians are now much stronger in the early game economically, they should now grow at a similar pace than human and other players too until the middle game where they will gradually fade. They should also spawn cities more often and be relevant longer in the game, even though for now at least if not always or not they have trouble to grow past a certain size, and are still not a real player (no diplomacy and scattered cities that are independent, but that contribute together to the "barbarian(s?)((,)'s) civilization"), and become a key part in politics or invasion decisions, maybe i mean or not or/and other or not anyways etc. Their (now new)(anyways etc) overall strength increase (for example they are also harder to kill at all difficulties (less to no combat bonus against them, but never reverse bonus to